    - region "Corporate culture":
      - heading "Corporate culture" [level=3]
      - figure "Co-founder Steve Wozniak and Macintosh engineer Andy Hertzfeld attended the Apple User Group Connection club in 1985.":
        - link:
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Steve_Wozniak_and_Andy_Hertzfeld_1985.jpg
          - img
        - text: Co-founder
        - link "Steve Wozniak":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Wozniak
        - text: and Macintosh engineer
        - link "Andy Hertzfeld":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andy_Hertzfeld
        - text: attended the
        - link "Apple User Group Connection":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_User_Group_Connection
        - text: club in 1985.
      - figure "Universities that produced the most alumni who became Apple employees":
        - link:
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Colleges_with_the_most_alumni_at_Apple.png
          - img
        - text: Universities that produced the most alumni who became Apple employees
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple is one of several highly successful companies founded in the 1970s that bucked the traditional notions of
        - link "corporate culture":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corporate_culture
        - text: . Jobs often walked around the office barefoot even after Apple became a
        - link "Fortune 500":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortune_500
        - text: company. By the time of the "
        - link "1984":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1984_(advertisement)
        - text: "\" television advertisement, Apple's informal culture had become a key trait that differentiated it from its competitors."
        - superscript:
          - link "[225]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-226"
        - text: According to a 2011 report in
        - link "Fortune":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortune_(magazine)
        - text: ", this has resulted in a corporate culture more akin to a startup rather than a multinational corporation."
        - superscript:
          - link "[226]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Fortune1-227"
        - text: In a 2017 interview, Wozniak credited watching
        - link "Star Trek":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Trek
        - text: and attending
        - link "Star Trek conventions":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Trek_convention
        - text: in his youth as inspiration for co-founding Apple.
        - superscript:
          - link "[227]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-228"
      - paragraph:
        - text: As the company has grown and been led by a series of differently opinionated chief executives, some media have suggested that it has lost some of its original character.
        - superscript:
          - link "[228]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-229"
        - superscript:
          - link "[229]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-230"
        - superscript:
          - link "[230]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-231"
        - superscript:
          - link "[231]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-232"
        - text: Nonetheless, it has maintained a reputation for fostering individuality and excellence that reliably attracts talented workers, particularly after Jobs returned.
        - superscript:
          - link "[232]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-233"
        - text: Numerous Apple employees have stated that projects without Jobs' involvement often took longer than others.
        - superscript:
          - link "[233]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-cultofmacwork-234"
      - paragraph:
        - text: The Apple
        - link "Fellows":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fellow#Industry_and_corporate_fellows
        - text: program awards employees for extraordinary technical or leadership contributions to
        - link "personal computing":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_computing
        - text: . Recipients include
        - link "Bill Atkinson":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_Atkinson
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[234]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-hertzfeld-235"
        - link "Steve Capps":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Capps
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[235]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-236"
        - link "Rod Holt":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rod_Holt
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[234]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-hertzfeld-235"
        - link "Alan Kay":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alan_Kay
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[236]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-yoyow-237"
        - superscript:
          - link "[237]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-238"
        - link "Guy Kawasaki":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guy_Kawasaki
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[236]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-yoyow-237"
        - superscript:
          - link "[238]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-239"
        - link "Al Alcorn":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al_Alcorn
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[239]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-240"
        - link "Don Norman":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Norman
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[236]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-yoyow-237"
        - link "Rich Page":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rich_Page
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[234]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-hertzfeld-235"
        - link "Steve Wozniak":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Wozniak
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[234]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-hertzfeld-235"
        - text: and
        - link "Phil Schiller":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phil_Schiller
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[240]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-241"
      - paragraph:
        - text: Jobs intended that employees were to be specialists who are not exposed to functions outside their area of expertise. For instance,
        - link "Ron Johnson":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ron_Johnson_(businessman)
        - text: —Senior Vice President of Retail Operations until November 1, 2011—was responsible for site selection, in-store service, and store layout, yet had no control of the inventory in his stores. This was done by Tim Cook, who had a background in supply-chain management.
        - superscript:
          - link "[241]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Lashinsky2011-242"
        - text: Apple is known for strictly enforcing accountability. Each project has a "directly responsible individual" or "DRI" in Apple jargon.
        - superscript:
          - link "[226]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Fortune1-227"
        - superscript:
          - link "[242]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-243"
        - text: Unlike other major US companies, Apple provides a relatively simple compensation policy for executives that does not include perks enjoyed by other CEOs like country club fees or private use of company aircraft. The company typically grants stock options to executives every other year.
        - superscript:
          - link "[243]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-244"
      - paragraph:
        - text: In 2015, Apple had 110,000 full-time employees. This increased to 116,000 full-time employees the next year, a notable hiring decrease, largely due to its first revenue decline. Apple does not specify how many of its employees work in retail, though its 2014
        - link "SEC":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Securities_and_Exchange_Commission
        - text: filing put the number at approximately half of its employee base.
        - superscript:
          - link "[244]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-245"
        - text: In September 2017, Apple announced that it had over 123,000 full-time employees.
        - superscript:
          - link "[245]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-246"
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple has a strong culture of
        - link "corporate secrecy":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corporate_secrecy
        - text: ", and has an anti-"
        - link "leak":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/News_leak
        - text: Global Security team that recruits from the
        - link "National Security Agency":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Security_Agency
        - text: ", the"
        - link "Federal Bureau of Investigation":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Bureau_of_Investigation
        - text: ", and the"
        - link "United States Secret Service":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Secret_Service
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[246]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-247"
        - text: In December 2017,
        - link "Glassdoor":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glassdoor
        - text: said Apple was the 48th best place to work, having originally entered at rank 19 in 2009, peaking at rank 10 in 2012, and falling down the ranks in subsequent years.
        - superscript:
          - link "[247]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-248"
        - text: In 2023, Bloomberg's
        - link "Mark Gurman":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Gurman_(journalist)
        - text: revealed the existence of Apple's Exploratory Design Group (XDG), which was working to add glucose monitoring to the Apple Watch. Gurman compared XDG to Alphabet's
        - link "X \"moonshot factory\"":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X_Development
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[248]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-249"
    - region "Criticism and controversies":
      - heading "Criticism and controversies" [level=3]
      - note:
        - text: "Main article:"
        - link "Criticism of Apple Inc.":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Criticism_of_Apple_Inc.
      - note:
        - text: "Further information:"
        - link "Apple litigation":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_litigation
        - text: ","
        - link "United States v. Apple (2024)":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_v._Apple_(2024)
        - text: ","
        - link "Apple–EU tax dispute":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple–EU_tax_dispute
        - text: ","
        - link "Foxconn suicides":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foxconn_suicides
        - text: ", and"
        - link "Apple in China":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_in_China
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple has faced criticism regarding its market practices, labor conditions, and privacy policies. The company has been criticized for alleged anti-competitive behavior
        - superscript:
          - link "[249]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-250"
        - text: and its alleged participation in the
        - link "PRISM":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PRISM
        - text: surveillance program.
        - superscript:
          - link "[250]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-251"
        - text: Significant concerns have been raised about labor issues in
        - link "its supply chain":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_supply_chain
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[251]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-252"
        - text: restrictions on the
        - link "right to repair":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right_to_repair
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[252]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-253"
        - text: and the company's tax strategies and environmental impact. Furthermore, Apple has faced scrutiny for its business operations in nations with restrictive
        - link "human rights records":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_rights_in_China
        - text: ", such as"
        - link "China":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[253]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-254"
      - paragraph:
        - text: On May 5, 2026, Apple settled for $250 million a shareholder lawsuit after the lawsuit was filed by Peter Landsheft in US federal court in California in 2024 over delays in AI upgrades to Siri.
        - superscript:
          - link "[254]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-255"
    - region "Offices":
      - heading "Offices" [level=3]
      - figure "Apple Infinite Loop campus in Cupertino":
        - link:
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Apple_Infinite_Loop_campus.jpg
          - img
        - link "Apple Infinite Loop campus":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Infinite_Loop_campus
        - text: in Cupertino
      - figure "Apple Wolfe campus in Sunnyvale":
        - link:
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Apple_Wolfe_campus.jpg
          - img
        - link "Apple Wolfe campus":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Wolfe_campus
        - text: in Sunnyvale
      - note:
        - text: "Main articles:"
        - link "Apple Park":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Park
        - text: ","
        - link "Apple Infinite Loop campus":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Infinite_Loop_campus
        - text: ", and"
        - link "Apple Wolfe campus":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Wolfe_campus
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple's headquarters are located at
        - link "Apple Park":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Park
        - text: in
        - link "Cupertino":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cupertino
        - text: ", in the center of"
        - link "Silicon Valley":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicon_Valley
        - text: . Opened in 2017, the circular
        - link "groundscraper":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groundscraper
        - text: houses more than 12,000 employees. Apple co-founder
        - link "Steve Jobs":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Jobs
        - text: envisioned the campus as resembling a nature preserve rather than a conventional office park, and presented the proposal to the Cupertino City Council in June 2011 during his final public appearance before his death.
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple also operates the
        - link "Infinite Loop campus":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Infinite_Loop_campus
        - text: ", approximately 1 mile (1.6 km) west of Apple Park."
        - superscript:
          - link "[255]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-256"
        - text: Opened in 1993 as a research and development campus, it served as Apple's headquarters from 1997 until the opening of Apple Park in 2017 and continues to house offices and laboratories.
      - paragraph:
        - text: In nearby
        - link "Sunnyvale, California":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunnyvale,_California
        - text: ", Apple occupies the"
        - link "Wolfe campus":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Wolfe_campus
        - text: ", which was completed in 2018 after the company acquired the office development during construction."
      - paragraph:
        - text: In addition to these campuses, Apple occupies numerous office and research buildings throughout Cupertino and Sunnyvale, including the former headquarters buildings Stephens Creek 3 (1977–1978) and Mariani 1 (1982–1993).
        - superscript:
          - link "[256]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-257"
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple's headquarters for Europe, the Middle East and Africa (EMEA) are located in
        - link "Cork":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cork_(city)
        - text: in the south of
        - link "Ireland":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_Ireland
        - text: ", called the Hollyhill campus."
        - superscript:
          - link "[257]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-258"
        - text: The facility, which opened in 1980, houses 5,500 people and was Apple's first location outside of the United States.
        - superscript:
          - link "[258]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-259"
        - text: Apple's international sales and distribution arms operate out of the campus in Cork.
        - superscript:
          - link "[259]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-260"
      - paragraph:
        - text: "Apple has two campuses near Austin, Texas: a 216,000-square-foot (20,100 m"
        - superscript: "2"
        - text: ) campus opened in 2014 houses 500 engineers who work on
        - link "Apple silicon":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_silicon
        - superscript:
          - link "[260]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-261"
        - text: and a 1.1-million-square-foot (100,000 m
        - superscript: "2"
        - text: ) campus opened in 2021 where 6,000 people work in technical support, supply chain management, online store curation, and
        - link "Apple Maps":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Maps
        - text: data management. The company also has several other major locations in
        - link "Boulder, Colorado":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boulder,_Colorado
        - text: ","
        - link "Culver City, California":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culver_City,_California
        - text: ","
        - link "Elk Grove, California":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elk_Grove,_California
        - text: ","
        - link "Herzliya":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herzliya
        - text: ","
        - link "London":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London
        - text: ","
        - link "Los Angeles":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Angeles
        - text: ","
        - link "Munich":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munich
        - text: ","
        - link "San Diego":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Diego
        - text: ","
        - link "Seattle":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seattle
        - text: ", and"
        - link "Shanghai":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shanghai
        - text: that each employ hundreds of people.
        - superscript:
          - link "[261]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-262"
    - region "Litigation":
      - heading "Litigation" [level=3]
      - note:
        - text: "Main article:"
        - link "Litigation involving Apple Inc.":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Litigation_involving_Apple_Inc.
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple has been a participant in various legal proceedings and claims since it began operation.
        - superscript:
          - link "[262]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-263"
        - text: In particular, Apple is known for and promotes itself as actively and aggressively enforcing its intellectual property interests. Some litigation examples include
        - link "Apple v. Samsung":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_v._Samsung
        - text: ","
        - link "Apple v. Microsoft":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_v._Microsoft
        - text: ","
        - link "Motorola Mobility v. Apple Inc.":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motorola_Mobility_v._Apple_Inc.
        - text: ", and"
        - link "Apple Corps v. Apple Computer":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Corps_v._Apple_Computer
        - text: . Apple has also had to defend itself against charges on numerous occasions of violating intellectual property rights. Most have been dismissed in the courts as
        - link "shell companies":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shell_companies
        - text: known as
        - link "patent trolls":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patent_troll
        - text: ", with no evidence of actual use of"
        - link "patents":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patent
        - text: in question.
        - superscript:
          - link "[263]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-264"
        - text: On December 21, 2016, Nokia announced that in the US and Germany, it has filed a suit against Apple, claiming that the latter's products infringe on Nokia's patents.
        - superscript:
          - link "[264]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-265"
      - paragraph:
        - text: Most recently, in November 2017, the
        - link "United States International Trade Commission":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_International_Trade_Commission
        - text: announced an investigation into allegations of patent infringement in regards to Apple's remote desktop technology; Aqua Connect, a company that builds remote desktop software, has claimed that Apple infringed on two of its patents.
        - superscript:
          - link "[265]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-266"
      - paragraph:
        - link "Epic Games":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epic_Games
        - text: filed a
        - link "lawsuit":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epic_Games_v._Apple
        - text: against Apple in August 2020 in the
        - link "United States District Court for the Northern District of California":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_District_Court_for_the_Northern_District_of_California
        - text: ", related to Apple's practices in the"
        - link "App Store for iOS":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/App_Store_(Apple)
        - text: .
      - paragraph:
        - text: In January 2022,
        - link "Ericsson":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ericsson
        - text: sued Apple over payment of royalty of
        - link "5G":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/5G
        - text: technology.
        - superscript:
          - link "[266]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-267"
        - text: On June 24, 2024, the
        - link "European Commission":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Commission
        - text: accused Apple of violating the
        - link "Digital Markets Act":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_Markets_Act
        - text: by preventing "app developers from freely steering consumers to alternative channels for offers and content".
        - superscript:
          - link "[267]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-268"
        - text: In April 2025, Apple was found guilty and fined €500 million ($570 million) for violating the Digital Markets Act.
        - superscript:
          - link "[268]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-269"
    - region "Lobbying":
      - heading "Lobbying" [level=3]
      - paragraph:
        - text: In 2025, Apple was one of the donors who funded the White House's
        - link "East Wing demolition":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Wing_demolition
        - text: ", and planned building of"
        - link "a ballroom":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House_State_Ballroom
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[269]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-270"
  - region "Finances":
    - heading "Finances" [level=2]
    - note:
      - text: "See also:"
      - link "List of mergers and acquisitions by Apple":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_mergers_and_acquisitions_by_Apple
    - table "Revenue by market (2024)[270]":
      - caption:
        - text: Revenue by market (2024)
        - superscript:
          - link "[270]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-271"
      - rowgroup:
        - row "Country Share":
          - columnheader "Country"
          - columnheader "Share"
        - row "Americas 43.7%":
          - cell "Americas":
            - link "Americas":
              - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Americas
          - cell "43.7%"
        - row "EMEA 25.9%":
          - cell "EMEA":
            - link "EMEA":
              - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EMEA
          - cell "25.9%"
        - row "Greater China 17.1%":
          - cell "Greater China":
            - link "Greater China":
              - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_China
          - cell "17.1%"
        - row "Rest of Asia-Pacific 7.8%":
          - cell "Rest of Asia-Pacific":
            - text: Rest of
            - link "Asia-Pacific":
              - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asia-Pacific
          - cell "7.8%"
        - row "Japan 6.1%":
          - cell "Japan"
          - cell "6.1%"
    - paragraph:
      - text: As of 2024, Apple was the world's
      - link "fourth-largest personal computer vendor":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Market_share_of_personal_computer_vendors#Current_top_vendors_market_share_(2023)
      - text: ","
      - superscript:
        - link "[271]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-272"
      - text: the
      - link "largest vendor of tablet computers":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tablet_computer#By_manufacturer
      - text: ","
      - superscript:
        - link "[272]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-273"
      - text: and the
      - link "largest vendor of mobile phones":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_best-selling_mobile_phones#2024
      - text: .
      - superscript:
        - link "[273]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-274"
      - text: It is a
      - link "Big Tech":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Tech
      - text: company.
      - superscript:
        - link "[274]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-275"
      - superscript:
        - link "[275]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-276"
    - paragraph:
      - text: In its fiscal year ending in September 2011, Apple reported a total of $108 billion in annual revenues—a significant increase from its 2010 revenues of $65 billion—and nearly $82 billion in
      - link "cash reserves":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cash_reserves
      - text: .
      - superscript:
        - link "[276]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-277"
      - text: On March 19, 2012, Apple announced plans for a $2.65-per-share
      - link "dividend":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dividend
      - text: beginning in the fourth quarter of 2012, per approval by its board of directors.
      - superscript:
        - link "[277]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-dividendfaq-278"
    - paragraph:
      - text: The company's worldwide annual revenue in 2013 totaled $170 billion.
      - superscript:
        - link "[278]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-279"
      - text: In May 2013, Apple entered the top ten of the
      - link "Fortune 500":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortune_500
      - text: list of companies for the first time, rising 11 places above its 2012 ranking to take the sixth position.
      - superscript:
        - link "[279]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-280"
      - text: As of 2016, Apple has around US$234 billion of cash and marketable securities, of which 90% is located outside the United States for tax purposes.
      - superscript:
        - link "[280]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-b200-281"
    - paragraph:
      - text: Apple amassed 65% of all profits made by the eight largest worldwide smartphone manufacturers in quarter one of 2014, according to a report by
      - link "Canaccord Genuity":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canaccord_Genuity
      - text: . In the first quarter of 2015, the company garnered 92% of all earnings.
      - superscript:
        - link "[281]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-282"
    - paragraph:
      - text: On April 30, 2017,
      - link "The Wall Street Journal":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Wall_Street_Journal
      - text: reported that Apple had
      - link "cash reserves":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cash_reserve
      - text: of $250 billion,
      - superscript:
        - link "[282]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-283"
      - text: officially confirmed by Apple as specifically $256.8 billion a few days later.
      - superscript:
        - link "[283]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-284"
    - paragraph:
      - text: As of August 3, 2018, Apple was the
      - link "largest publicly traded corporation in the world by market capitalization":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_corporations_by_market_capitalization
      - text: . On August 2, 2018, Apple became the first publicly traded US company to reach a $1 trillion market value,
      - superscript:
        - link "[284]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-1t-CNBC-285"
      - superscript:
        - link "[285]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-1t-Guardian-286"
      - text: and, as of October 2025, is valued at just over $4 trillion.
      - superscript:
        - link "[286]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-287"
      - text: Apple was ranked No. 3 on the 2023
      - link "Fortune 500":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortune_500
      - text: rankings of the
      - link "largest United States corporations by revenue":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_largest_companies_in_the_United_States_by_revenue
      - text: .
      - superscript:
        - link "[287]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-288"
    - paragraph:
      - text: In July 2022, Apple reported an 11% decline in Q3 profits compared to 2021. Its revenue in the same period rose 2% year-on-year to $83 billion, though this figure was also lower than in 2021, where the increase was at 36%. The general downturn is reportedly caused by the slowing global economy and supply chain disruptions in China.
      - superscript:
        - link "[288]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-289"
      - text: That year, Apple was one of the
      - link "largest corporate spenders":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_companies_by_research_and_development_spending
      - text: on research and development worldwide, with R&D expenditure amounting to over $27 billion.
      - superscript:
        - link "[289]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-290"
    - paragraph:
      - text: In May 2023, Apple reported a decline in its sales for the first quarter of 2023. Compared to that of 2022, revenue for 2023 fell by 3%. This is Apple's second consecutive quarter of sales decline. This fall is attributed to the slowing economy and consumers putting off purchases of iPads and computers due to increased pricing. However, iPhone sales held up with a year-on-year increase of 1.5%. According to Apple, demands for such devices were strong, particularly in Latin America and South Asia.
      - superscript:
        - link "[290]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-291"
    - region "Taxes":
      - heading "Taxes" [level=3]
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple has created subsidiaries in low-tax places such as
        - link "Ireland":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_Ireland
        - text: ", the"
        - link "Netherlands":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netherlands
        - text: ","
        - link "Luxembourg":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luxembourg
        - text: ", and the"
        - link "British Virgin Islands":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Virgin_Islands
        - text: to cut the taxes it pays around the world. According to
        - link "The New York Times":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times
        - text: ", in the 1980s Apple was among the first tech companies to designate overseas salespeople in high-tax countries in a manner that allowed the company to sell on behalf of low-tax subsidiaries on other continents, sidestepping income taxes. In the late 1980s, Apple was a pioneer of an accounting technique known as the \""
        - link "Double Irish with a Dutch sandwich":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double_Irish_with_a_Dutch_sandwich
        - text: "\", which reduces taxes by routing profits through Irish subsidiaries and the Netherlands and then to the Caribbean."
        - superscript:
          - link "[291]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-292"
        - superscript:
          - link "[292]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-293"
      - paragraph:
        - link "British Conservative Party":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Conservative_Party
        - text: Member of Parliament
        - link "Charlie Elphicke":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlie_Elphicke
        - text: published research on October 30, 2012,
        - superscript:
          - link "[293]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-294"
        - text: which showed that some multinational companies, including Apple, were making billions of pounds of profit in the UK, but were paying an effective tax rate to the UK Treasury of only 3 percent, well below standard
        - link "corporate tax":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corporate_tax
        - text: rates. He followed this research by calling on the
        - link "Chancellor of the Exchequer":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chancellor_of_the_Exchequer
        - link "George Osborne":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Osborne
        - text: to force these multinationals, which also included
        - link "Google":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google
        - text: and
        - link "the Coca-Cola Company":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Coca-Cola_Company
        - text: ", to state the effective rate of tax it pays on its UK revenues. Elphicke also said that government contracts should be withheld from multinationals who do not pay their fair share of UK tax."
        - superscript:
          - link "[294]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-295"
      - paragraph:
        - text: According to a
        - link "US Senate":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_Senate
        - text: report on the company's offshore tax structure concluded in May 2013, Apple has held billions of dollars in profits in Irish subsidiaries to pay little or no taxes to any government by using an unusual global tax structure.
        - superscript:
          - link "[295]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-296"
        - text: The main subsidiary, a holding company that includes Apple's retail stores throughout Europe, has not paid any corporate income tax in the last five years. "Apple has exploited a difference between Irish and U.S. tax residency rules", the report said.
        - superscript:
          - link "[296]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-297"
        - text: On May 21, 2013, Apple CEO
        - link "Tim Cook":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tim_Cook
        - text: defended his company's tax tactics at a Senate hearing.
        - superscript:
          - link "[297]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-298"
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple says that it is the single largest taxpayer in the US, with an
        - link "effective tax rate":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tax_rate
        - text: of approximately of 26% as of Q2
        - link "FY":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fiscal_year
        - text: "2016."
        - superscript:
          - link "[298]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-299"
        - text: In an interview with the German newspaper
        - link "FAZ":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankfurter_Allgemeine_Zeitung
        - text: in October 2017, Tim Cook stated that Apple was the biggest taxpayer worldwide.
        - superscript:
          - link "[299]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-300"
      - paragraph:
        - text: In 2016, after
        - link "a two-year investigation":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ireland_v_Commission
        - text: ", the European Commission claimed that Apple's use of a hybrid"
        - link "Double Irish tax arrangement":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double_Irish_arrangement
        - text: constituted "illegal state aid" from Ireland, and ordered Apple to pay €13 billion ($14.5 billion) in unpaid taxes, the largest corporate tax fine in history. This was later annulled, after the
        - link "European General Court":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_General_Court
        - text: ruled that the commission had provided insufficient evidence.
        - superscript:
          - link "[300]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-301"
        - superscript:
          - link "[301]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-IT11-302"
        - text: In 2018, Apple repatriated $285 billion to the United States, resulting in a $38-billion tax payment spread over the following eight years.
        - superscript:
          - link "[302]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-303"
      - table "Apple's effective tax rate in %[205][206]":
        - caption:
          - text: Apple's effective tax rate in %
          - superscript:
            - link "[205]":
              - /url: "#cite_note-investor-relations-205"
          - superscript:
            - link "[206]":
              - /url: "#cite_note-sec-fillings-206"
