    - region "Charity":
      - heading "Charity" [level=3]
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple is a partner of
        - link "Product Red":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Product_Red
        - text: ", a fundraising campaign for"
        - link "AIDS":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AIDS
        - text: charity. In November 2014, Apple arranged for all
        - link "App Store":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/App_Store_(iOS)
        - text: revenue in a two-week period to go to the fundraiser,
        - superscript:
          - link "[303]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-304"
        - text: generating more than US$20 million,
        - superscript:
          - link "[304]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-305"
        - text: and in March 2017, it released an
        - link "iPhone 7":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPhone_7
        - text: with a red color finish.
        - superscript:
          - link "[305]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-306"
        - text: As of 2021, Apple has donated over $250 million to Product Red.
        - superscript:
          - link "[306]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-307"
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple contributes financially to fundraisers in times of natural disasters. In November 2012, it donated $2.5 million to the
        - link "American Red Cross":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Red_Cross
        - text: to aid relief efforts after
        - link "Hurricane Sandy":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Sandy
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[307]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-308"
        - text: and in 2017 it donated $5 million to relief efforts for both
        - link "Hurricane Irma":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Irma
        - text: and
        - link "Hurricane Harvey":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Harvey
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[308]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-309"
        - text: and for the
        - link "2017 Central Mexico earthquake":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017_Central_Mexico_earthquake
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[309]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-310"
        - text: The company has used its
        - link "iTunes":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ITunes
        - text: platform to encourage donations in the wake of environmental disasters and humanitarian crises, such as the
        - link "2010 Haiti earthquake":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2010_Haiti_earthquake
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[310]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-311"
        - text: the
        - link "2011 Japan earthquake":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2011_Japan_earthquake
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[311]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-312"
        - link "Typhoon Haiyan":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Typhoon_Haiyan
        - text: in the Philippines in November 2013,
        - superscript:
          - link "[312]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-313"
        - text: and the
        - link "2015 European migrant crisis":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2015_European_migrant_crisis
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[313]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-314"
        - text: Apple emphasizes that it does not incur any processing or other fees for iTunes donations, sending 100% of the payments directly to relief efforts, though it also acknowledges that the Red Cross does not receive any personal information on the users donating and that the payments may not be tax deductible.
        - superscript:
          - link "[314]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-315"
      - paragraph:
        - text: On April 14, 2016, Apple and the
        - link "World Wide Fund for Nature":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Wide_Fund_for_Nature
        - text: (WWF) announced that they have engaged in a partnership to, "help protect life on our planet". Apple released a special page in its
        - link "App Store":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/App_Store_(Apple)
        - text: ", Apps for Earth. In the arrangement, Apple has committed that through April 24, WWF will receive 100% of the proceeds from the applications participating in the App Store via both the purchases of any paid apps and the In-App Purchases. Apple and WWF's Apps for Earth campaign raised more than $8 million in total proceeds to support WWF's conservation work. WWF announced the results at WWDC 2016 in San Francisco."
        - superscript:
          - link "[315]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-316"
      - paragraph:
        - text: During the
        - link "COVID-19 pandemic":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic
        - text: ", Apple's CEO Cook announced that the company would be donating \"millions\" of masks to health workers in the United States and Europe."
        - superscript:
          - link "[316]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-317"
        - text: On January 13, 2021, Apple announced a $100-million Racial Equity and Justice Initiative to help combat
        - link "institutional racism":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Institutional_racism
        - text: worldwide after the 2020
        - link "murder of George Floyd":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murder_of_George_Floyd
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[317]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Doubles-318"
        - superscript:
          - link "[318]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-319"
        - superscript:
          - link "[319]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-320"
        - text: In June 2023, Apple announced that it was doubling this and then distributed more than $200 million to support organizations focused on education, economic growth, and criminal justice. Half is philanthropic grants and half is centered on equity.
        - superscript:
          - link "[317]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Doubles-318"
  - region "Environment":
    - heading "Environment" [level=2]
    - note:
      - text: "Main article:"
      - link "Environmental impact of Apple Inc.":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environmental_impact_of_Apple_Inc.
    - region "Apple Energy":
      - heading "Apple Energy" [level=3]
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple Energy, LLC is a wholly owned subsidiary of Apple that sells
        - link "solar energy":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_energy
        - text: . As of June 6, 2016, Apple's solar farms in California and Nevada have been declared to provide 217.9 megawatts of solar generation capacity.
        - superscript:
          - link "[320]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-321"
        - text: Apple has received regulatory approval to construct a
        - link "landfill gas energy plant":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landfill_gas_utilization
        - text: in
        - link "North Carolina":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Carolina
        - text: to use the
        - link "methane emissions":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Methane_emissions
        - text: to generate electricity.
        - superscript:
          - link "[321]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-322"
        - text: Apple's North Carolina data center is already powered entirely by renewable sources.
        - superscript:
          - link "[322]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-323"
    - region "Energy and resources":
      - heading "Energy and resources" [level=3]
      - paragraph:
        - text: In 2010,
        - link "Climate Counts":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_Counts
        - text: ", a nonprofit organization dedicated to directing consumers toward the greenest companies, gave Apple a score of 52 points out of a possible 100, which puts Apple in its top category \"Striding\"."
        - superscript:
          - link "[323]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-324"
        - text: This was an increase from May 2008, when Climate Counts only gave Apple 11 points out of 100, which placed the company last among electronics companies, at which time Climate Counts also labeled Apple with a "stuck icon", adding that Apple at the time was "a choice to avoid for the climate-conscious consumer".
        - superscript:
          - link "[324]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-iwclimate-325"
      - paragraph:
        - text: Following a
        - link "Greenpeace":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenpeace
        - text: protest, Apple released a statement on April 17, 2012, committing to ending its use of coal and shifting to 100% renewable clean energy.
        - superscript:
          - link "[325]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-After_Greenpeace_Protests,_Apple_Promises_to_Dump_Coal_Power-326"
        - superscript:
          - link "[326]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Powering_Our_Facilities_with_Clean,_Renewable_Energy-327"
        - text: By 2013, Apple was using
        - link "100% renewable energy":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/100%25_renewable_energy
        - text: to power its
        - link "data centers":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_centers
        - text: . Overall, 75% of the company's power came from clean renewable sources.
        - superscript:
          - link "[327]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-328"
      - paragraph:
        - text: In May 2015, Greenpeace evaluated the state of the Green Internet and commended Apple on its environmental practices saying, "Apple's commitment to renewable energy has helped set a new bar for the industry, illustrating in very concrete terms that a 100% renewable Internet is within its reach, and providing several models of intervention for other companies that want to build a sustainable Internet."
        - superscript:
          - link "[328]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-329"
      - paragraph:
        - text: As of 2016, Apple states that 100% of its US operations run on
        - link "renewable energy":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Renewable_energy
        - text: ", 100% of Apple's data centers run on renewable energy and 93% of Apple's global operations run on renewable energy."
        - superscript:
          - link "[329]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-330"
        - text: However, the facilities are connected to the local
        - link "grid":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrical_grid
        - text: which usually contains a mix of fossil and renewable sources, so Apple uses
        - link "carbon offsets":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon_offset
        - text: for its electricity use.
        - superscript:
          - link "[330]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-331"
        - text: The
        - link "Electronic Product Environmental Assessment Tool":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electronic_Product_Environmental_Assessment_Tool
        - text: (EPEAT) allows consumers to see the effect a product has on the environment. Each product receives a Gold, Silver, or Bronze rank depending on its efficiency and sustainability. Every Apple
        - link "tablet":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tablet_computer
        - text: ","
        - link "notebook":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laptop
        - text: ","
        - link "desktop computer":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desktop_computer
        - text: ", and"
        - link "display":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_monitor
        - text: that EPEAT ranks achieves a Gold rating, the highest possible. Although Apple's data centers recycle water 35 times,
        - superscript:
          - link "[331]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-332"
        - text: the increased activity in retail, corporate and data centers also increase the amount of water use to 573 million US gal (2.2 million m
        - superscript: "3"
        - text: ) in 2015.
        - superscript:
          - link "[332]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-333"
      - paragraph:
        - text: During an event on March 21, 2016, Apple provided a status update on its environmental initiative to be 100% renewable in all of its worldwide operations.
        - link "Lisa P. Jackson":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lisa_P._Jackson
        - text: ", Apple's vice president of Environment, Policy and Social Initiatives who reports directly to CEO, Tim Cook, announced that as of March 2016, 93% of Apple's worldwide operations are powered with renewable energy. Also featured was the company's efforts to use sustainable paper in its product packaging; 99% of all paper used by Apple in the product packaging comes from"
        - link "post-consumer":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Post-consumer
        - link "recycled":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recycle
        - text: paper or sustainably managed forests, as the company continues its move to all paper packaging for all of its products.
        - superscript:
          - link "[333]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-334"
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple announced on August 16, 2016, that Lens Technology, one of its major suppliers in China, has committed to power all its
        - link "glass production":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass_production
        - text: for Apple with 100 percent renewable energy by 2018. The commitment was a large step in Apple's efforts to help manufacturers lower their carbon footprint in China.
        - superscript:
          - link "[334]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-335"
        - text: Apple also announced that all 14 of its final assembly sites in China are now compliant with
        - link "UL":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UL_(safety_organization)
        - text: "'s Zero Waste to Landfill validation. The standard, which started in January 2015, certifies that all manufacturing waste is"
        - link "reused":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reuse
        - text: ","
        - link "recycled":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recycled
        - text: ","
        - link "composted":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compost
        - text: ", or converted into energy (when necessary). Since the program began, nearly 140,000 metric tons of waste have been diverted from"
        - link "landfills":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landfill
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[335]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-336"
      - paragraph:
        - text: On July 21, 2020, Apple announced its plan to become
        - link "carbon neutral":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon_neutral
        - text: "across its entire business, manufacturing supply chain, and product life cycle by 2030. In the next 10 years, Apple will try to lower emissions with a series of innovative actions, including: low carbon product design, expanding energy efficiency, renewable energy, process and material innovations, and carbon removal."
        - superscript:
          - link "[336]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-337"
      - paragraph:
        - text: In June 2024, the
        - link "United States Environmental Protection Agency":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Environmental_Protection_Agency
        - text: (EPA) published a report about an electronic computer manufacturing facility leased by Apple in 2015 in
        - link "Santa Clara, California":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santa_Clara,_California
        - text: ", code named Aria."
        - superscript:
          - link "[337]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Roscoe20240625-338"
        - superscript:
          - link "[338]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-339"
        - text: The EPA report stated that Apple was potentially in violation of federal regulations under the
        - link "Resource Conservation and Recovery Act":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resource_Conservation_and_Recovery_Act
        - text: (RCRA).
        - superscript:
          - link "[337]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Roscoe20240625-338"
        - text: According to a report from
        - link "Bloomberg":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloomberg_News
        - text: in 2018, the facility is used to develop
        - link "microLED":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MicroLED
        - text: screens under the code name T159.
        - superscript:
          - link "[339]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-340"
        - superscript:
          - link "[337]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Roscoe20240625-338"
        - superscript:
          - link "[340]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-341"
        - text: The inspection found that Apple was potentially mistreating waste as only subject to
        - link "California":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/California
        - text: regulations and that it had potentially miscalculated the effectiveness of Apple's
        - link "activated carbon":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Activated_carbon
        - text: filters, which filter
        - link "volatile organic compounds":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volatile_organic_compound
        - text: (VOCs) from the air. The EPA inspected the facility in August 2023 due to a tip from a former Apple employee who posted the report on
        - link "X":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X_(social_media_platform)
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[337]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Roscoe20240625-338"
    - region "Toxins":
      - heading "Toxins" [level=3]
      - paragraph:
        - text: Following further campaigns by Greenpeace,
        - superscript:
          - link "[341]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-342"
        - text: in 2008, Apple became the first electronics manufacturer to eliminate all
        - link "polyvinyl chloride":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polyvinyl_chloride
        - text: (PVC) and
        - link "brominated flame retardants":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brominated_flame_retardant
        - text: (BFRs) in its complete product line.
        - superscript:
          - link "[342]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-343"
        - text: In June 2007, Apple began replacing the
        - link "cold cathode":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold_cathode
        - text: fluorescent lamp (CCFL) backlit
        - link "LCDs":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LCD
        - text: in its computers with
        - link "mercury":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercury_(element)
        - text: "-free"
        - link "LED-backlit LCD":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LED-backlit_LCD_display
        - text: and
        - link "arsenic":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arsenic
        - text: "-free glass, starting with the upgraded"
        - link "MacBook Pro":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacBook_Pro
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[343]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Apple-environmental-news-344"
        - superscript:
          - link "[344]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-agreenapple-345"
        - superscript:
          - link "[345]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-green_notebooks-346"
        - superscript:
          - link "[346]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-347"
        - text: Apple offers comprehensive and transparent information about the
        - link "CO2e":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon_dioxide_equivalent
          - text: CO
          - subscript: "2"
          - text: e
        - text: ","
        - link "emissions":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenhouse_gas_emissions
        - text: ", materials, and"
        - link "electrical usage":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_energy_consumption
        - text: concerning every product it currently produces or has sold in the past (and which it has enough data needed to produce the report), in its portfolio on its homepage. Allowing consumers to make informed purchasing decisions on the products it offers for sale.
        - superscript:
          - link "[347]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Apple-environment-reports-348"
        - text: In June 2009, Apple's
        - link "iPhone 3GS":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPhone_3GS
        - text: was free of PVC, arsenic, and BFRs.
        - superscript:
          - link "[343]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Apple-environmental-news-344"
        - superscript:
          - link "[348]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-349"
        - text: Since 2009, all Apple products have mercury-free LED-backlit LCDs, arsenic-free glass, and non-PVC cables.
        - superscript:
          - link "[349]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-350"
        - text: All Apple products have EPEAT Gold status and beat the latest Energy Star guidelines in each product's respective regulatory category.
        - superscript:
          - link "[343]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Apple-environmental-news-344"
        - superscript:
          - link "[350]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-351"
      - paragraph:
        - text: In November 2011, Apple was featured in Greenpeace's Guide to Greener Electronics, which ranks electronics manufacturers on sustainability,
        - link "climate and energy":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_and_energy
        - text: policy, and how "green" its products are. The company ranked fourth of fifteen electronics companies (moving up five places from the previous year) with a score of 4.6/10.
        - superscript:
          - link "[351]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-352"
        - text: Greenpeace praised Apple's
        - link "sustainability":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sustainability_metrics_and_indices
        - text: ", noting that the company exceeded its 70% global recycling goal in 2010. Apple continues to score well on product ratings, with all of its products now being free of PVC plastic and BFRs. However, the guide criticized Apple on the Energy criteria for not seeking external verification of its greenhouse gas emissions data, and for not setting any targets to reduce emissions."
        - superscript:
          - link "[352]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-353"
        - text: In January 2012, Apple requested that its cable maker, Volex, begin producing
        - link "halogen-free":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Low_smoke_zero_halogen
        - link "USB":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USB
        - text: and power cables.
        - superscript:
          - link "[353]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-354"
    - region "Green bonds":
      - heading "Green bonds" [level=3]
      - paragraph:
        - text: In February 2016, Apple issued a US$1.5-billion
        - link "green bond":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_bond
        - text: (climate bond), the first ever of its kind by a US tech company. The green bond proceeds are dedicated to the financing of environmental projects.
        - superscript:
          - link "[347]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Apple-environment-reports-348"
        - superscript:
          - link "[354]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-355"
  - region "Supply chain":
    - heading "Supply chain" [level=2]
    - note:
      - text: "Main article:"
      - link "Apple supply chain":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_supply_chain
    - note:
      - text: "See also:"
      - link "List of Apple Inc. suppliers":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Apple_Inc._suppliers
    - paragraph:
      - text: As of 2018, Apple used hardware components from 43 different countries.
      - superscript:
        - link "[355]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-356"
    - paragraph:
      - text: Apple products were made in the United States in Apple-owned factories until the late 1990s; however, as a result of
      - link "outsourcing":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outsourcing
      - text: initiatives in the 2000s, almost all of its manufacturing is now handled abroad. According to a report by The New York Times, Apple insiders "believe the vast scale of overseas factories, as well as the flexibility, diligence and industrial skills of foreign workers, have so outpaced their American counterparts that '
      - link "Made in the U.S.A.":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Made_in_the_USA
      - text: "' is no longer a viable option for most Apple products\"."
      - superscript:
        - link "[356]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-357"
      - text: South Korean firms, including
      - link "Samsung":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samsung
      - text: and
      - link "LG":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LG
      - text: ", worked closely with Apple as parts partners for the"
      - link "iMac":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IMac
      - text: ","
      - link "iPod":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPod
      - text: ", and"
      - link "iPhone":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPhone
      - text: before it increased its reliance on Taiwan and China.
      - superscript:
        - link "[357]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-:0-358"
      - superscript: ": 74–232"
      - text: Taiwanese Apple suppliers such as
      - link "Foxconn":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foxconn
      - text: ","
      - link "Wistron":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wistron
      - text: ","
      - link "Pegatron":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pegatron
      - text: ","
      - link "Quanta":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quanta_Computer
      - text: ", and"
      - link "Compal Electronics":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compal_Electronics
      - text: established a sizable manufacturing presence in China.
      - superscript:
        - link "[358]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-359"
      - text: By the 2020s, orders were shifting to Chinese firms such as to
      - link "Luxshare":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luxshare
      - text: ","
      - link "BYD Electronic":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BYD_Company
      - text: ","
      - link "Goertek":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goertek
      - text: ", and"
      - link "Wingtech":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wingtech
      - text: . By 2021, Apple had more suppliers from China than Taiwan.
      - superscript:
        - link "[357]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-:0-358"
      - superscript: ": 337"
      - text: In March 2017,
      - link "The Wall Street Journal":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Wall_Street_Journal
      - text: reported that Apple would begin manufacturing
      - link "iPhone models":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPhone_models
      - text: in India "over the next two months",
      - superscript:
        - link "[359]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-360"
      - text: and in May, the Journal wrote that an Apple manufacturer had begun production of
      - link "iPhone SE":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPhone_SE_(1st_generation)
      - text: in the country,
      - superscript:
        - link "[360]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-361"
      - text: In May 2017, the company announced a $1-billion funding project for "advanced manufacturing" in the United States,
      - superscript:
        - link "[361]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-362"
      - superscript:
        - link "[362]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-363"
      - text: In April 2019, Apple initiated manufacturing of
      - link "iPhone 7":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPhone_7
      - text: at its
      - link "Bengaluru":
        - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bengaluru
      - text: facility.
      - superscript:
        - link "[363]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-364"
    - paragraph:
      - text: "The company's manufacturing, procurement, and logistics enable it to execute massive product launches without having to maintain large, profit-sapping inventories. In 2011, Apple's profit margins were 40 percent, compared with between 10 and 20 percent for most other hardware companies. Cook's catchphrase to describe his focus on the company's operational arm is: \"Nobody wants to buy sour milk.\""
      - superscript:
        - link "[364]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-supply-365"
      - superscript:
        - link "[365]":
          - /url: "#cite_note-366"
    - region "Labor force":
      - heading "Labor force" [level=3]
      - note:
        - text: "Main article:"
        - link "Apple Inc. and unions":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Inc._and_unions
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple directly employs 147,000 workers including 25,000 corporate employees in
        - link "Apple Park":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Park
        - text: and across Silicon Valley.
        - superscript:
          - link "[366]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Downey2021-367"
        - superscript:
          - link "[367]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Leswing2021-368"
        - text: The vast majority of its employees work at the over 500 retail
        - link "Apple Stores":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Store
        - text: globally.
        - superscript:
          - link "[368]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-369"
        - text: Apple relies on a larger, outsourced workforce for manufacturing, particularly in China where Apple directly employs 10,000 workers across its retail and corporate divisions. In addition, one further million workers are contracted by Apple's suppliers to assemble Apple products, including
        - link "Foxconn":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foxconn
        - text: and
        - link "Pegatron":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pegatron
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[369]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-370"
        - link "Zhengzhou Technology Park":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhengzhou_Technology_Park
        - text: alone employs 350,000 Chinese workers in
        - link "Zhengzhou":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhengzhou
        - text: to exclusively work on the
        - link "iPhone":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPhone
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[370]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-371"
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple workers around the globe have been involved in
        - link "organizing":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organizing_model
        - text: since the 1990s.
        - superscript:
          - link "[371]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Hyde2002-372"
        - text: Apple unions are made up of retail, corporate, and outsourced workers. Apple employees have joined
        - link "trade unions":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade_union
        - text: or formed
        - link "works councils":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Works_council
        - text: in
        - link "Australia":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Inc._and_unions#Australia
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[372]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Zhuang2022-373"
        - link "France":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Inc._and_unions#France
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[373]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-374"
        - link "Germany":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Inc._and_unions#Germany
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[374]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Loehne2012-375"
        - link "Italy":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Inc._and_unions#Italy
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[375]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-376"
        - link "Japan":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Inc._and_unions#Japan
        - text: ","
        - superscript:
          - link "[376]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Corrales2022-377"
        - text: the
        - link "United Kingdom":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Inc._and_unions#United_Kingdom
        - superscript:
          - link "[377]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-378"
        - text: and the
        - link "United States":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Inc._and_unions#United_States
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[378]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Albergotti2022-379"
        - text: In 2021,
        - link "Apple Together":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Inc._and_unions#Apple_Together
        - text: ", a"
        - link "solidarity union":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solidarity_unionism
        - text: ", sought to bring together the company's global worker organizations."
        - superscript:
          - link "[379]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Harrington2022-380"
        - text: The majority of industrial
        - link "labor disputes":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Labor_dispute
        - text: (including union recognition) involving Apple occur indirectly through
        - link "its suppliers and contractors":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_supply_chain
        - text: ", notably"
        - link "Foxconn":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foxconn
        - text: plants in
        - link "China":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Inc._and_unions#China
        - superscript:
          - link "[380]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-Chan2013-381"
        - text: and, to a lesser extent, in
        - link "Brazil":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Inc._and_unions#Brazil
        - superscript:
          - link "[381]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-382"
        - text: and
        - link "India":
          - /url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Inc._and_unions#India
        - text: .
        - superscript:
          - link "[382]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-383"
      - paragraph:
        - text: Apple has been criticized for labor sourcing and conditions at the facilities of its contract manufacturers
        - superscript:
          - link "[383]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-384"
        - superscript:
          - link "[384]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-:02-385"
        - superscript:
          - link "[385]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-386"
        - text: as well as mines.
        - superscript:
          - link "[386]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-387"
        - superscript:
          - link "[387]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-388"
        - superscript:
          - link "[388]":
            - /url: "#cite_note-389"
